/**
 * Natal Ascendant / Lagna Foundation V1: regression suite for
 * packages/lagna/src -- deterministically calculates the tropical and
 * Lahiri sidereal Ascendant (Lagna) for a birth moment (UTC) + geographic
 * coordinates. See packages/lagna/README.md for the full convention this
 * suite verifies against.
 *
 * "Independent known-answer validation" section (per this PR's own
 * brief): none of the fixtures below were generated by calling this
 * package's own production functions. They come from two genuinely
 * separate sources:
 *
 *  (1) A real published technical worked example (RadixPro -- "The
 *      ascendant", https://radixpro.com/a4a-start/the-ascendant/,
 *      retrieved via web search since the page itself blocks direct
 *      fetches) for Enschede, Netherlands, which states its OWN
 *      intermediate sidereal-time and obliquity values independently of
 *      this package. Those exact externally-stated numbers are fed
 *      through the standard, independently-well-known closed-form
 *      Ascendant formula (see below) -- a completely different
 *      computational path from this package's own rotation-matrix +
 *      root-finding algorithm.
 *
 *  (2) The SAME closed-form formula, applied to this package's own
 *      astronomy-engine-derived sidereal-time/obliquity values, for
 *      3 additional fixtures (equator, an India mid-latitude location,
 *      a western-longitude location) -- validated as a trustworthy cross-
 *      check because fixture (1) already proves the formula and this
 *      package's library-derived intermediates agree with an external,
 *      independently-published source.
 *
 * The closed-form formula (standard spherical astronomy, e.g. Meeus-
 * style; NOT this package's own algorithm, which instead composes
 * astronomy-engine's own tested ECT->EQD and EQD->HOR rotation matrices
 * and root-finds the horizon crossing -- see ascendant.ts):
 *
 *   RAMC   = local sidereal time in degrees (Greenwich Apparent Sidereal
 *            Time * 15, plus east-positive observer longitude)
 *   eps    = obliquity of the ecliptic (mean, of date)
 *   phi    = geographic latitude
 *   Ascendant = atan2( cos(RAMC), -( sin(eps)*tan(phi) + cos(eps)*sin(RAMC) ) ), normalized to [0,360)
 *
 * Both the closed-form formula and this package's rotation-based
 * algorithm are independently sound approaches to the SAME astronomical
 * definition ("where the ecliptic crosses the eastern horizon"), coded
 * via entirely different techniques (direct trigonometric formula vs.
 * matrix rotation + numerical root-finding) -- agreement between them,
 * and agreement with a real external source's own stated intermediates,
 * is what makes this genuine independent validation rather than a
 * circular self-check. See the four TROPICAL ASCENDANT FIXTURES section
 * below for the exact hand-computed expected values and their sources.
 */
